WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.059
Book Two, Proposition Twelve.

2
00:00:02.059 --> 00:00:21.789
In obtuse-angled triangles, the square on the side subtending the obtuse angle is greater than the squares on the sides containing the obtuse angle by twice the rectangle contained by one of the sides about the obtuse angle, namely that on which the perpendicular falls, and the segment cut off outside by the perpendicular towards the obtuse angle.

3
00:00:21.789 --> 00:00:25.690
Let A B C be an obtuse-angled triangle having the angle B A C obtuse.

4
00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:32.135
Produce C A in a straight line, and from B draw B D perpendicular to C A produced.

5
00:00:32.135 --> 00:00:44.920
Since the segment C D has been cut at random at A, by Two Four the square on C D equals the squares on C A and A D together with twice the rectangle contained by C A and A D.

6
00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:47.580
Add the square on D B to each.

7
00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:56.090
Then the squares on C D and D B equal the squares on C A, A D, and D B together with twice the rectangle C A, A D.

8
00:00:56.090 --> 00:01:08.109
Because the angle at D is right, by One Forty-Seven the square on C B equals the squares on C D and D B, and the square on A B equals the squares on A D and D B.

9
00:01:08.109 --> 00:01:22.143
Therefore, the square on B C equals the squares on B A and A C together with twice the rectangle contained by C A and A D—and so exceeds the squares on B A and A C by twice that rectangle.

10
00:01:22.143 --> 00:01:23.860
Which was to be proved.

11
00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:33.744
Proposition Two Twelve is the Euclidean Law of Cosines for obtuse triangles: c squared equals a squared plus b squared plus two a times the exterior projection p.

12
00:01:33.744 --> 00:01:43.091
As Heath notes, Gregory of Saint Vincent also proved this theorem directly by the area-shearing method of One Forty-Seven, while Heron established its converse.

13
00:01:43.091 --> 00:01:53.840
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G ninety-four synthesizes nine foundational rules across fifty-two ancestor nodes, uniting Book One's Pythagorean theorem with Book Two's square of a sum.

14
00:01:54.260 --> 00:02:08.388
